${"##"} Sleep retention

Conditions:

* In these rows, a pad is any MIO pad `p` or any DIO pad `d`.
* A pad's status bit is the pad's bit in `MIO_PAD_SLEEP_STATUS` or in `DIO_PAD_SLEEP_STATUS`.
* Sleep entry is a rise of `sleep_en_i` from 0 to 1.
* The rows for sleep modes 0 to 3 ${"hold while no TAP owns the pad and the pad's sleep mode has not changed" if strap else "hold while the pad's sleep mode has not changed"} since sleep entry.

Testpoints: [`pad_sleep`](../../data/pinmux_env_fpv_testplan.hjson#pad_sleep) (V2, priority 2), [`sleep_release`](../../data/pinmux_env_fpv_testplan.hjson#sleep_release) (V2, priority 2).

| ID | Requirement |
|---|---|
| <a id="req_slp_01"></a>REQ_SLP_01 | <br>At sleep entry, the status bit of every pad whose sleep enable is 1 shall become 1. [`REQ_SLP_09`](#req_slp_09) states the one exception, in which a software clear of that bit lands in the same cycle. <br><br>*Sources:* [`MIO_PAD_SLEEP_EN.EN`](../registers.md#mio_pad_sleep_en); [Sleep Features](../programmers_guide.md#sleep-features)<br><br>*Checked by:* `pad_sleep`: *bit set at entry*. At every sleep entry in which the pad's sleep enable is 1 and software does not clear the status bit in that cycle, the check requires the bit to be 1 in the next cycle.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_slp_02"></a>REQ_SLP_02 | <br>A status bit shall become 1 only at sleep entry, and only if the pad's sleep enable is 1. <br><br>*Sources:* [`MIO_PAD_SLEEP_STATUS.EN`](../registers.md#mio_pad_sleep_status${on(topname in 'earlgrey englishbreakfast'.split(), '_0')}); [Sleep Features](../programmers_guide.md#sleep-features)<br><br>*Checked by:* `pad_sleep`: *bit set only at entry*. Whenever a status bit rises from 0 to 1, the check requires the cycle before to be a sleep entry in which the pad's sleep enable is 1.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_slp_03a"></a>REQ_SLP_03a | <br>While a pad is asleep and its sleep mode is 0, the pad's output shall be 0. <br><br>*Sources:* [`MIO_PAD_SLEEP_MODE.OUT`](../registers.md#mio_pad_sleep_mode); [Sleep Features](../programmers_guide.md#sleep-features)<br><br>*Checked by:* `pad_sleep`: *tie low in sleep*. While a pad's status bit is 1 and its sleep mode was 0 at the sleep entry, the check compares the pad's output with 0 in the same cycle.${' The check skips every cycle in which a TAP owns the pad.' if strap else ''}<br><br> |
| <a id="req_slp_03b"></a>REQ_SLP_03b | <br>While a pad is asleep and its sleep mode is 0, the pad's output enable shall be 1, so the pad drives 0. <br><br>*Sources:* [`MIO_PAD_SLEEP_MODE.OUT`](../registers.md#mio_pad_sleep_mode); [Sleep Features](../programmers_guide.md#sleep-features)<br><br>*Checked by:* `pad_sleep`: *tie low in sleep*. While a pad's status bit is 1 and its sleep mode was 0 at the sleep entry, the check compares the pad's output enable with 1.${' The check skips every cycle in which a TAP owns the pad.' if strap else ''}<br><br> |
| <a id="req_slp_04a"></a>REQ_SLP_04a | <br>While a pad is asleep and its sleep mode is 1, the pad's output shall be 1. <br><br>*Sources:* [`MIO_PAD_SLEEP_MODE.OUT`](../registers.md#mio_pad_sleep_mode)<br><br>*Checked by:* `pad_sleep`: *tie high in sleep*. While a pad's status bit is 1 and its sleep mode was 1 at the sleep entry, the check compares the pad's output with 1 in the same cycle.${' The check skips every cycle in which a TAP owns the pad.' if strap else ''}<br><br> |
| <a id="req_slp_04b"></a>REQ_SLP_04b | <br>While a pad is asleep and its sleep mode is 1, the pad's output enable shall be 1, so the pad drives 1. <br><br>*Sources:* [`MIO_PAD_SLEEP_MODE.OUT`](../registers.md#mio_pad_sleep_mode)<br><br>*Checked by:* `pad_sleep`: *tie high in sleep*. While a pad's status bit is 1 and its sleep mode was 1 at the sleep entry, the check compares the pad's output enable with 1.${' The check skips every cycle in which a TAP owns the pad.' if strap else ''}<br><br> |
| <a id="req_slp_05a"></a>REQ_SLP_05a | <br>While a pad is asleep and its sleep mode is 2, the pad's output enable shall be 0, so the pad is high-Z. <br><br>*Sources:* [`MIO_PAD_SLEEP_MODE.OUT`](../registers.md#mio_pad_sleep_mode)<br><br>*Checked by:* `pad_sleep`: *high-Z in sleep*. While a pad's status bit is 1 and its sleep mode was 2 at the sleep entry, the check compares the pad's output enable with 0 in the same cycle.${' The check skips every cycle in which a TAP owns the pad.' if strap else ''}<br><br> |
| <a id="req_slp_05b"></a>REQ_SLP_05b | <br>While a pad is asleep and its sleep mode is 2, the pad's output shall be 0. <br><br>*Sources:* [`MIO_PAD_SLEEP_MODE.OUT`](../registers.md#mio_pad_sleep_mode)<br><br>*Checked by:* `pad_sleep`: *high-Z in sleep*. While a pad's status bit is 1 and its sleep mode was 2 at the sleep entry, the check compares the pad's output with 0.${' The check skips every cycle in which a TAP owns the pad.' if strap else ''}<br><br> |
| <a id="req_slp_06a"></a>REQ_SLP_06a | <br>While a pad is asleep and its sleep mode is 3, the pad shall keep the output that it drove in the last cycle before its status bit became 1. <br><br>*Sources:* [`MIO_PAD_SLEEP_MODE.OUT`](../registers.md#mio_pad_sleep_mode); [Retention Logic](../theory_of_operation.md#retention-logic)<br><br>*Checked by:* `pad_sleep`: *keep holds*. While a pad's status bit is 1 and its sleep mode was 3 at the sleep entry that set the bit, the check compares the pad's output with its output in that entry cycle.${' The check applies only if no TAP owned the pad then and no TAP owns it now.' if strap else ''}<br><br> |
| <a id="req_slp_06b"></a>REQ_SLP_06b | <br>While a pad is asleep and its sleep mode is 3, the pad shall keep the output enable that it drove in the last cycle before its status bit became 1. <br><br>*Sources:* [`MIO_PAD_SLEEP_MODE.OUT`](../registers.md#mio_pad_sleep_mode); [Retention Logic](../theory_of_operation.md#retention-logic)<br><br>*Checked by:* `pad_sleep`: *keep holds*. While a pad's status bit is 1 and its sleep mode was 3 at the sleep entry that set the bit, the check compares the pad's output enable with its enable in that entry cycle.${' The check applies only if no TAP owned the pad then and no TAP owns it now.' if strap else ''}<br><br> |
| <a id="req_slp_07"></a>REQ_SLP_07 | <br>Once a status bit is 1, the bit shall stay 1 until software writes 0 to it. The rule holds at every value of `sleep_en_i` and of the pad's sleep enable, and for every value that the peripheral drives. <br><br>*Sources:* [`MIO_PAD_SLEEP_STATUS.EN`](../registers.md#mio_pad_sleep_status${on(topname in 'earlgrey englishbreakfast'.split(), '_0')}); [Retention Logic](../theory_of_operation.md#retention-logic)<br><br>*Checked by:* `pad_sleep`: *bit holds until cleared*, *clear releases*. If software does not clear a status bit at 1 in a cycle, the check _bit holds until cleared_ requires the bit to be 1 in the next cycle. The check _clear releases_ requires the bit to be 0 in the cycle after a clear.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_slp_08"></a>REQ_SLP_08 | <br>While an MIO pad is awake, the pad shall drive what its output select names. While a DIO pad is awake, the pad shall drive the output and output enable of its own peripheral. <br><br>*Sources:* [Retention Logic](../theory_of_operation.md#retention-logic); [Sleep Features](../programmers_guide.md#sleep-features)<br><br>*Checked by:* `pad_output`: *tie 0 drives 0*, *tie 1 drives 1*, *select 2 is high-Z*, *drives selected output*, *select without an output is high-Z*, *DIO drives its peripheral*. Each of these checks compares the pad's drive with its selected drive in every cycle in which the pad's status bit is 0.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_slp_09"></a>REQ_SLP_09 | <br>When sleep entry sets a status bit in the cycle that software writes 0 to that bit, the bit shall end at 0. The software clear has precedence over sleep entry. <br><br>*Sources:* [`MIO_PAD_SLEEP_STATUS`](../registers.md#mio_pad_sleep_status${on(topname in 'earlgrey englishbreakfast'.split(), '_0')}); [Type RW](../../../../../../util/reggen/README.md#type-rw); [Simultaneous SW and HW access](../../../../../../util/reggen/README.md#simultaneous-sw-and-hw-access)<br><br>*Checked by:* `pad_sleep`: *clear wins*. When a sleep entry with the pad's sleep enable at 1 falls in the same cycle as a software clear of the status bit, the check requires the bit to be 0 in the next cycle.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_slp_10a"></a>REQ_SLP_10a | <br>A write to a pad's sleep mode while the pad is asleep shall leave the pad as it is. <br><br>*Sources:* [Retention Logic](../theory_of_operation.md#retention-logic); [Sleep Features](../programmers_guide.md#sleep-features)<br><br>*Checked by:* `pad_sleep`: *mode write while held*. After software writes the pad's sleep mode while the status bit is 1, the check requires the pad to keep the sleep value of the mode taken at the sleep entry.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_slp_10b"></a>REQ_SLP_10b | <br>After a write to a pad's sleep mode while the pad is asleep, the new mode shall take effect at the next sleep entry. <br><br>*Sources:* [Retention Logic](../theory_of_operation.md#retention-logic); [Sleep Features](../programmers_guide.md#sleep-features)<br><br>*Checked by:* `pad_sleep`: *tie low in sleep*, *tie high in sleep*, *high-Z in sleep*, *keep holds*; [CSR checker](README.md#csr-checker). Each of the four sleep mode checks uses the sleep mode that the pad had at the sleep entry. The CSR checker checks that the register keeps the written mode.<br><br> |

${"##"} Register locks

Security-relevant ([Groups](#groups)).

Conditions:

* A "lock" is one bit of a `*_REGWEN` register.
* Each lock locks the registers that have the same index.
* A lock is open while it is 1 and closed while it is 0.
* The rule that a locked pad's attribute output does not change ([`REQ_LOCK_03a`](#req_lock_03a)) holds on every pad${', except on a JTAG pad while a TAP owns that pad.' if strap else '.'}
* The rule for the value that software reads ([`REQ_LOCK_03b`](#req_lock_03b)) holds on every pad.

Testpoints: [`register_block`](../../data/pinmux_env_fpv_testplan.hjson#register_block) (V2, priority 2), [`regwen`](../../data/pinmux_env_fpv_testplan.hjson#regwen) (V2, priority 2).

| ID | Requirement |
|---|---|
| <a id="req_lock_01a"></a>REQ_LOCK_01a | <br>Every lock shall read 1 after reset. <br><br>*Sources:* the register description: the `EN` field of each `*_REGWEN`, for example [`MIO_PAD_ATTR_REGWEN.EN`](../registers.md#mio_pad_attr_regwen) `resval: "1"`, on `clk_i` with `rst_ni` ([pinmux.hjson `clocking`](../../data/pinmux.hjson)); [Configuration and Register Definition File Format](../../../../../../util/reggen/README.md#configuration-and-register-definition-file-format)<br><br>*Checked by:* [CSR checker](README.md#csr-checker). After reset, the CSR checker expects every lock to be 1, and compares the lock's value and each read of it with 1. Bit 31 of `ALERT_TEST` locks the alert test in the same way, and the CSR checker expects it to be 1 after reset too.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_lock_01b"></a>REQ_LOCK_01b | <br>Once software writes 0 to a lock, the lock shall stay 0 until reset. <br><br>*Sources:* [Configuration and Register Definition File Format](../../../../../../util/reggen/README.md#configuration-and-register-definition-file-format); [Pad Attributes](../programmers_guide.md#pad-attributes)<br><br>*Checked by:* [CSR checker](README.md#csr-checker). The CSR checker predicts each lock as an `rw0c` field. In that prediction, an accepted write of 0 clears the lock and nothing sets it. A lock that returns to 1 before a reset fails the comparison.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_lock_01c"></a>REQ_LOCK_01c | <br>A write of 1 to a lock that is 0 shall not reopen it. <br><br>*Sources:* the register description: each lock `rw0c`; [Configuration and Register Definition File Format](../../../../../../util/reggen/README.md#configuration-and-register-definition-file-format); [Pinmux Configuration](../programmers_guide.md#pinmux-configuration)<br><br>*Checked by:* [CSR checker](README.md#csr-checker). The CSR checker predicts each lock as an `rw0c` field, so a closed lock stays 0 on a write of 1. A lock that such a write reopens fails the comparison.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_lock_02"></a>REQ_LOCK_02 | <br>While a lock is 0, a write to a register it locks shall leave that register unchanged. <br><br>*Sources:* [`MIO_PERIPH_INSEL_REGWEN.EN`](../registers.md#mio_periph_insel_regwen); [Configuration and Register Definition File Format](../../../../../../util/reggen/README.md#configuration-and-register-definition-file-format)<br><br>*Checked by:* `regwen`: *locked read-back holds*; [CSR checker](README.md#csr-checker). The CSR checker predicts that a register whose lock reads 0 keeps its value over every write. For the attribute registers, the check _locked read-back holds_ requires the read-back to stay unchanged while the lock reads 0.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_lock_03a"></a>REQ_LOCK_03a | <br>While a pad's attribute lock is 0, the pad's attribute output shall not change. <br><br>*Sources:* [`MIO_PAD_ATTR_REGWEN.EN`](../registers.md#mio_pad_attr_regwen); [Pad Attributes](../programmers_guide.md#pad-attributes)<br><br>*Checked by:* `regwen`: *locked attribute holds*. While the attribute lock of a pad is closed, the check requires the pad's attribute output to keep its value from each cycle to the next. ${'The check covers every pad that no TAP owns.' if strap else 'The check covers every pad.'}<br><br> |
| <a id="req_lock_03b"></a>REQ_LOCK_03b | <br>While a pad's attribute lock is 0, the value that software reads from the pad's attribute register shall not change. <br><br>*Sources:* [`MIO_PAD_ATTR_REGWEN.EN`](../registers.md#mio_pad_attr_regwen); [Pad Attributes](../programmers_guide.md#pad-attributes)<br><br>*Checked by:* `regwen`: *locked read-back holds*. While the attribute lock of a pad is closed, the check requires the pad's read-back to keep its value from each cycle to the next. ${'The check covers every pad, including a JTAG pad that a TAP owns.' if strap else 'The check covers every pad.'}<br><br> |

${"##"} Register block and bus

Security-relevant ([Groups](#groups)).

Conditions:

* A "failed request" is a request whose command integrity code does not match, or whose data integrity code does not match ([Usage of User Bits](../../../../../ip/tlul/README.md#usage-of-user-bits)).
* No document says whether the data integrity code of a read is checked, because that code covers the write data ([Usage of User Bits](../../../../../ip/tlul/README.md#usage-of-user-bits)).
* These rows follow the design, which checks the data integrity code of every request.
* The pinmux's alert sender answers a ping of the alert handler with an alert event, which is a full handshake on the alert and acknowledge pairs.
* The alert handler takes that handshake as the answer to its ping (${'' if topname in 'earlgrey darjeeling'.split() else "Earl Grey's "}[Ping Testing](../../../../../top_${topname if topname in 'earlgrey darjeeling'.split() else 'earlgrey'}/ip_autogen/alert_handler/doc/theory_of_operation.md#ping-testing)).
* The "in-band reset" is the alert handler's re-initialization.
* During the in-band reset, the alert handler puts a signal integrity error on its ping and acknowledge pairs on purpose (${'' if topname in 'earlgrey darjeeling'.split() else "Earl Grey's "}[Employed Solution](../../../../../top_${topname if topname in 'earlgrey darjeeling'.split() else 'earlgrey'}/ip_autogen/alert_handler/doc/theory_of_operation.md#employed-solution)).
* A request has an "illegal combination of address, size and mask" when its address is not aligned to its size, when its size is greater than 2, or when its mask has a bit set outside the access.
* A `PutFullData` request also has an illegal combination when the mask bit of a lane inside the access is 0.

Testpoints: [`register_block`](../../data/pinmux_env_fpv_testplan.hjson#register_block) (V2, priority 2), [`bus`](../../data/pinmux_env_fpv_testplan.hjson#bus) (V2, priority 2), [`alert`](../../data/pinmux_env_fpv_testplan.hjson#alert) (V2S, priority 2).

| ID | Requirement |
|---|---|
| <a id="req_reg_01a"></a>REQ_REG_01a | <br>Every register field shall behave as its access type requires on each software read or write that is answered without an error. This row covers a write only while the field's lock reads 1, or when the field has no lock. <br><br>*Sources:* the register description: each field's `swaccess`, for example [`MIO_PAD_SLEEP_STATUS`](../registers.md#mio_pad_sleep_status${on(topname in 'earlgrey englishbreakfast'.split(), '_0')}) `swaccess: "rw0c"`; [Configuration and Register Definition File Format](../../../../../../util/reggen/README.md#configuration-and-register-definition-file-format)<br><br>*Checked by:* `pad_attr`: *read-back changes only on a write*; [CSR checker](README.md#csr-checker). For the attribute registers, the check _read-back changes only on a write_ allows a read-back to change only in the cycle after an attribute write. The CSR checker predicts each field from its access type.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_reg_01b"></a>REQ_REG_01b | <br>After reset, every register field shall read the reset value that the register description gives the field. <br><br>*Sources:* the register description: each field's `resval`, for example [`MIO_PAD_SLEEP_STATUS.EN`](../registers.md#mio_pad_sleep_status${on(topname in 'earlgrey englishbreakfast'.split(), '_0')}) `resval: "0"`; [Configuration and Register Definition File Format](../../../../../../util/reggen/README.md#configuration-and-register-definition-file-format)<br><br>*Checked by:* [CSR checker](README.md#csr-checker). After reset, the CSR checker requires each field to hold its reset value. The check _reset attributes_ covers the reset values of the attribute registers.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_bus_01a"></a>REQ_BUS_01a | <br>The pinmux shall answer every request with one response, and only one. (*RTL*: the limit of one response per request) <br><br>*Sources:* TileLink 1.9.3 p. 39: "Receipt of request messages must result in the eventual receipt of a response message by the requestor."; p. 30: "it is illegal to receive a response without receiving the request."; p. 46: "In TL-UL, every message fits within a single beat". No specification states the limit of one response per request.<br><br>*Checked by:* `bus`: *device rules*. The check uses the device rules of the TL-UL ABVIP on the pinmux's port. These rules match each response to one open request.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_bus_01b"></a>REQ_BUS_01b | <br>The pinmux shall answer every request with a response that follows the TL-UL rules and carries the source ID of the request. <br><br>*Sources:* [Source ID requirements for device elements](../../../../../ip/tlul/README.md#source-id-requirements-for-device-elements)<br><br>*Checked by:* `bus`: *device rules*. The check uses the device rules of the TL-UL ABVIP on the pinmux's port. These rules compare the `d_source` of each response with the `a_source` of its request.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_bus_01c"></a>REQ_BUS_01c | <br>A request shall receive an error response only if it is a failed request, a request that one of [`REQ_BUS_10a`](#req_bus_10a) to [`REQ_BUS_10d`](#req_bus_10d) describes, or a request to an address that no register maps. (*RTL*: the error on a read whose only mismatch is in its data integrity code, and the error on an address that is not aligned to the request's size) <br><br>*Sources:* [Explicit Error Cases](../../../../../ip/tlul/README.md#explicit-error-cases); [Error responses](../../../../../../util/reggen/README.md#error-responses). No specification states the error on a read whose only mismatch is in its data integrity code, and the error on an address that is not aligned to the request's size.<br><br>*Checked by:* `bus`: *errors as the register checker predicts*; [CSR checker](README.md#csr-checker). For every request that is not a failed request, the error checks of the CSR checker require `d_error` to be 1 exactly when the request has one of the listed causes.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_bus_02a"></a>REQ_BUS_02a | <br>Every response shall carry the response integrity code, which is computed over the op code, the size and the error of that response. (*RTL*: the encoding of the response integrity code) <br><br>*Sources:* [Usage of User Bits](../../../../../ip/tlul/README.md#usage-of-user-bits); [pinmux.hjson `countermeasures` BUS.INTEGRITY](../../data/pinmux.hjson). No specification states the encoding of the response integrity code.<br><br>*Checked by:* `bus`: *response codes correct*. For each response, the check recomputes the response code from the response's opcode, size and error with the integrity functions of `tlul_pkg`. The check then compares the result with the response code that the pinmux sends.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_bus_02b"></a>REQ_BUS_02b | <br>Every response shall carry the data integrity code, which is computed over the data of that response. (*RTL*: the encoding of the data integrity code) <br><br>*Sources:* [Usage of User Bits](../../../../../ip/tlul/README.md#usage-of-user-bits); [pinmux.hjson `countermeasures` BUS.INTEGRITY](../../data/pinmux.hjson). No specification states the encoding of the data integrity code.<br><br>*Checked by:* `bus`: *response codes correct*. For each response, the check recomputes the data code over the response data with the integrity functions of `tlul_pkg`. The check then compares the result with the data code that the pinmux sends.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_bus_03"></a>REQ_BUS_03 | <br>A failed request shall receive an error response. (*RTL*) <br><br>*Sources:* No specification states this rule. Nearest text: [Usage of User Bits](../../../../../ip/tlul/README.md#usage-of-user-bits); [cip_base_vseq](../../../../../dv/sv/cip_lib/README.md#cip_base_vseq)<br><br>*Checked by:* `bus`: *failed request errors*. The check requires `d_error` to be 1 on the response to every failed request.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_bus_04"></a>REQ_BUS_04 | <br>A write that is a failed request shall change no register. (*RTL*) <br><br>*Sources:* No specification states this rule. Nearest text: [Usage of User Bits](../../../../../ip/tlul/README.md#usage-of-user-bits)<br><br>*Checked by:* `bus`: *failed write changes nothing*. The check requires every register to keep its value over a failed write. The one exception is an AON field that is still taking an earlier write, which has not landed yet.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_bus_05"></a>REQ_BUS_05 | <br>Outside the alert handler's in-band reset, a failed request shall raise the fatal alert `fatal_fault`. (*RTL*: the alert for a read whose only mismatch is in its data integrity code) <br><br>*Sources:* [pinmux.hjson `alert_list` fatal_fault](../../data/pinmux.hjson); ${'' if topname in 'earlgrey darjeeling'.split() else "Earl Grey's "}[Employed Solution](../../../../../top_${topname if topname in 'earlgrey darjeeling'.split() else 'earlgrey'}/ip_autogen/alert_handler/doc/theory_of_operation.md#employed-solution). No specification states the alert for a read whose only mismatch is in its data integrity code.<br><br>*Checked by:* `alert`: *failed request alerts*. The check requires an alert event on `alert_tx_o` within 40 cycles of every failed request.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_bus_06"></a>REQ_BUS_06 | <br>Outside the alert handler's in-band reset, the pinmux shall send the fatal alert repeatedly from the time it is raised until reset. <br><br>*Sources:* [Alerts per Module](../../../../../../doc/contributing/hw/comportability/README.md#alerts-per-module); ${'' if topname in 'earlgrey darjeeling'.split() else "Earl Grey's "}[Employed Solution](../../../../../top_${topname if topname in 'earlgrey darjeeling'.split() else 'earlgrey'}/ip_autogen/alert_handler/doc/theory_of_operation.md#employed-solution)<br><br>*Checked by:* `alert`: *fatal alert repeats*. After a failed request and until reset, the check requires every cycle to be followed by an alert event within 40 cycles.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_bus_07"></a>REQ_BUS_07 | <br>While bit 31 of `ALERT_TEST` is 1, a write of 1 to bit 0 of `ALERT_TEST` that is answered without an error shall send one alert event. The rule applies only outside the alert handler's in-band reset. <br><br>*Sources:* The register page says of bit 0: "Write 1 to trigger one alert event of this kind." ([`ALERT_TEST`](../registers.md#alert_test)); [Test Alert Register Creation](../../../../../../doc/contributing/hw/comportability/README.md#test-alert-register-creation); ${'' if topname in 'earlgrey darjeeling'.split() else "Earl Grey's "}[Employed Solution](../../../../../top_${topname if topname in 'earlgrey darjeeling'.split() else 'earlgrey'}/ip_autogen/alert_handler/doc/theory_of_operation.md#employed-solution)<br><br>*Checked by:* `alert`: *test sends an event*, *no alert without a cause*. The check _test sends an event_ requires an alert event within 40 cycles of each alert test. In the check _no alert without a cause_, each alert test allows one alert event, so one test gives one event.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_bus_08"></a>REQ_BUS_08 | <br>While bit 31 of `ALERT_TEST` is 0, a write to bit 0 of `ALERT_TEST` that is not a failed request shall send no alert. The rule applies only outside the alert handler's in-band reset. <br><br>*Sources:* The register page says of bit 31: "Write 0 to disable alert testing until the next reset." ([`ALERT_TEST`](../registers.md#alert_test)); ${'' if topname in 'earlgrey darjeeling'.split() else "Earl Grey's "}[Employed Solution](../../../../../top_${topname if topname in 'earlgrey darjeeling'.split() else 'earlgrey'}/ip_autogen/alert_handler/doc/theory_of_operation.md#employed-solution)<br><br>*Checked by:* `alert`: *no alert without a cause*. The check allows no alert event for a write of bit 0 while bit 31 of `ALERT_TEST` is 0. An alert event that has no other cause then fails the check.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_bus_09"></a>REQ_BUS_09 | <br>Outside the alert handler's in-band reset, the pinmux shall send the fatal alert only after a failed request or an alert test. This rule does not cover the pinmux's answers to the alert handler's pings. <br><br>*Sources:* [pinmux.hjson `alert_list` fatal_fault](../../data/pinmux.hjson); ${'' if topname in 'earlgrey darjeeling'.split() else "Earl Grey's "}[Employed Solution](../../../../../top_${topname if topname in 'earlgrey darjeeling'.split() else 'earlgrey'}/ip_autogen/alert_handler/doc/theory_of_operation.md#employed-solution)<br><br>*Checked by:* `alert`: *no alert without a cause*. The check allows an alert event only after a failed request, after a ping or after an alert test. Each ping and each alert test allows one event.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_bus_10a"></a>REQ_BUS_10a | <br>A request with an undefined opcode shall receive an error response. <br><br>*Sources:* [Usage of Opcode, Size and Mask](../../../../../ip/tlul/README.md#usage-of-opcode-size-and-mask); [Error responses](../../../../../../util/reggen/README.md#error-responses)<br><br>*Checked by:* `bus`: *errors as the register checker predicts*; [CSR checker](README.md#csr-checker). The error checks of the CSR checker require `d_error` to be 1 on a request with an undefined opcode. The proof lets the opcode take any value.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_bus_10b"></a>REQ_BUS_10b | <br>A request with an illegal combination of address, size and mask shall receive an error response. These combinations are defined in the Conditions above. (*RTL*: the error on an address that is not aligned to the request's size) <br><br>*Sources:* [Explicit Error Cases](../../../../../ip/tlul/README.md#explicit-error-cases); TileLink 1.9.3 p. 51: "a_address must be aligned to a_size.". No specification states the error on an address that is not aligned to the request's size.<br><br>*Checked by:* `bus`: *errors as the register checker predicts*; [CSR checker](README.md#csr-checker). The error checks of the CSR checker require `d_error` to be 1 on a request with an illegal combination of address, size and mask. The proof keeps every mask contiguous.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_bus_10c"></a>REQ_BUS_10c | <br>A write of 8 or 16 bits to an address not aligned to a word shall receive an error response. <br><br>*Sources:* [Error responses](../../../../../../util/reggen/README.md#error-responses); [Explicit Error Cases](../../../../../ip/tlul/README.md#explicit-error-cases)<br><br>*Checked by:* `bus`: *errors as the register checker predicts*; [CSR checker](README.md#csr-checker). The error checks of the CSR checker require `d_error` to be 1 on an 8-bit or 16-bit write that is not aligned to a word.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_bus_10d"></a>REQ_BUS_10d | <br>A write narrower than its register shall receive an error response. The width of a register is set by the location of its fields. <br><br>*Sources:* [Error responses](../../../../../../util/reggen/README.md#error-responses)<br><br>*Checked by:* `bus`: *errors as the register checker predicts*; [CSR checker](README.md#csr-checker). The error checks of the CSR checker require `d_error` to be 1 on a write that is narrower than its register.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_bus_10e"></a>REQ_BUS_10e | <br>A request to an address that no register maps shall receive an error response, when that address is within the address bits that the register block decodes. <br><br>*Sources:* [Explicit Error Cases](../../../../../ip/tlul/README.md#explicit-error-cases); [Error responses](../../../../../../util/reggen/README.md#error-responses)<br><br>*Checked by:* `bus`: *errors as the register checker predicts*; [CSR checker](README.md#csr-checker). The error checks of the CSR checker require `d_error` to be 1 on a request to an address that maps to no register.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_bus_11"></a>REQ_BUS_11 | <br>A write that is answered with an error for any reason other than a failed integrity check shall change no register. <br><br>*Sources:* [cip_base_vseq](../../../../../dv/sv/cip_lib/README.md#cip_base_vseq); [Error responses](../../../../../../util/reggen/README.md#error-responses)<br><br>*Checked by:* `bus`: *error write changes nothing*; [CSR checker](README.md#csr-checker). A write answered with an error is not an accepted write. The CSR checker and the no-write checks of the attribute registers and the AON fields therefore require every register to keep its value over that write.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_bus_12"></a>REQ_BUS_12 | <br>Outside the alert handler's in-band reset, the pinmux shall answer each ping of the alert handler with one alert event within a bounded time. <br><br>*Sources:* ${'' if topname in 'earlgrey darjeeling'.split() else "Earl Grey's "}[Ping Testing](../../../../../top_${topname if topname in 'earlgrey darjeeling'.split() else 'earlgrey'}/ip_autogen/alert_handler/doc/theory_of_operation.md#ping-testing)<br><br>*Checked by:* `alert`: *ping answered*, *no alert without a cause*. The check _ping answered_ requires an alert event within 40 cycles of each ping. In the check _no alert without a cause_, each ping allows one alert event, so one ping gives one event.<br><br> |

${"##"} Wakeup detectors

Conditions:

* The detector index `w` runs from 0 to `NWkupDetect` - 1, and a cycle in these rows is a cycle of `clk_aon_i`.
* The watched signal and the forwarded value are defined in the [Terms](#terms).
* The register values in these rows are those that the AON logic holds once a write has crossed into the AON domain.
* The rows hold while an enabled detector keeps its mode.
* The other fields of the detector may change while it is enabled, although no specification states what such a change does.
* The rows hold at every value of `sleep_en_i` and of the scan mode, and also while `clk_i` is stopped.
% if scan_clk:
* One exception: the rows do not hold while scan mode is on and the detector watches a scan clock pad.
% endif
* The rows are checked from the filter's fourth sample after the AON reset `rst_aon_ni`.
* The timed rows ([`REQ_WKUP_09a`](#req_wkup_09a) to [`REQ_WKUP_10b`](#req_wkup_10b)) hold while `CNT_TH` is 1 or greater.

Testpoints: [`wakeup`](../../data/pinmux_env_fpv_testplan.hjson#wakeup) (V2, priority 1), [`wakeup_cause`](../../data/pinmux_env_fpv_testplan.hjson#wakeup_cause) (V2, priority 2), [`wakeup_sync`](../../data/pinmux_env_fpv_testplan.hjson#wakeup_sync) (V2, priority 2).

| ID | Requirement |
|---|---|
| <a id="req_wkup_01"></a>REQ_WKUP_01 | <br>While a detector's `MIODIO` is 0, its watched signal shall be the input of MIO pad `PADSEL` - 2 for `PADSEL` from 2 to `NMioPads` + 1, the constant 0 for `PADSEL` 0 and the constant 1 for `PADSEL` 1. <br><br>*Sources:* [`MIO_PERIPH_INSEL.IN`](../registers.md#mio_periph_insel); [`WKUP_DETECTOR.MIODIO`](../registers.md#wkup_detector); [`WKUP_DETECTOR_PADSEL.SEL`](../registers.md#wkup_detector_padsel); [Retention and Wakeup Features](../theory_of_operation.md#retention-and-wakeup-features); [Wakeup Detectors](../theory_of_operation.md#wakeup-detectors); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `n_mio_pads`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup`: *edge sets cause*, *timed high sets cause*, *timed low sets cause*, *no false cause*. The checks watch the MIO pad or the constant that the select names.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_02"></a>REQ_WKUP_02 | <br>While a detector's `MIODIO` is 1, its watched signal shall be the input of DIO pad `PADSEL` for `PADSEL` from 0 to `NDioPads` - 1. <br><br>*Sources:* [`WKUP_DETECTOR.MIODIO`](../registers.md#wkup_detector); [`WKUP_DETECTOR_PADSEL.SEL`](../registers.md#wkup_detector_padsel); [MIO and DIO Signal Categories](../theory_of_operation.md#mio-and-dio-signal-categories); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `n_dio_pads`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup`: *edge sets cause*, *no false cause*. The checks watch the forwarded value of the DIO pad that the select names.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_03"></a>REQ_WKUP_03 | <br>A detector's watched signal shall be 0 while its `PADSEL` names no pad. `PADSEL` names no pad when it is greater than `NMioPads` + 1 and `MIODIO` is 0, or when it is `NDioPads` or greater and `MIODIO` is 1. <br><br>*Sources:* [`WKUP_DETECTOR_PADSEL.SEL`](../registers.md#wkup_detector_padsel); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `n_mio_pads`, `n_dio_pads`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup`: *timed low sets cause*, *no false cause*. On a select that names no pad, the checks watch the value 0. The checks therefore require a detector in mode 4 to detect, and allow no edge to set the cause bit.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_04"></a>REQ_WKUP_04 | <br>While a detector's `FILTER` is 0, its forwarded value shall be the watched signal as sampled at each `clk_aon_i` edge. <br><br>*Sources:* [Wakeup Detectors](../theory_of_operation.md#wakeup-detectors)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup`: *edge sets cause*, *no false cause*. With `FILTER` 0, the forwarded value that the checks watch is the sampled pad. The checks allow the pad to be seen up to 4 cycles late.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_05"></a>REQ_WKUP_05 | <br>While a detector's `FILTER` is 1, its forwarded value shall take a new value only once 4 consecutive samples of the watched signal have had that value. <br><br>*Sources:* [`WKUP_DETECTOR.FILTER`](../registers.md#wkup_detector)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup`: *edge sets cause*, *no false cause*. The checks count a level that holds for 4 consecutive samples as an edge of the forwarded value. A glitch of 3 samples gives no edge.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_06a"></a>REQ_WKUP_06a | <br>An enabled detector in mode 0 (Posedge) shall detect every rise of the forwarded value. <br><br>*Sources:* [`WKUP_DETECTOR.MODE`](../registers.md#wkup_detector); [Wakeup Detectors](../theory_of_operation.md#wakeup-detectors)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup`: *posedge sets cause*. The check requires a hardware write of 1 to the cause bit within 8 cycles of each rise of the forwarded value, if `EN` is 1 at the rise and in each of the 4 cycles after it. A pulse of one sample counts as a rise.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_06b"></a>REQ_WKUP_06b | <br>An enabled detector in mode 0 (Posedge) shall detect only at a rise of the forwarded value. <br><br>*Sources:* [`WKUP_DETECTOR.MODE`](../registers.md#wkup_detector); [Wakeup Detectors](../theory_of_operation.md#wakeup-detectors)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup`: *no false cause*. In mode 0, the check allows a hardware write of 1 to a cause bit at 0 only when the write answers a rise of the forwarded value with `EN` 1. The check allows the rise to be seen up to 4 cycles late.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_07a"></a>REQ_WKUP_07a | <br>An enabled detector in mode 1 (Negedge) shall detect every fall of the forwarded value. <br><br>*Sources:* [`WKUP_DETECTOR.MODE`](../registers.md#wkup_detector)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup`: *negedge sets cause*. The check requires a hardware write of 1 to bit `w` within 8 cycles of each fall of the forwarded value, if `EN` is 1 at the fall and in each of the 4 cycles after it.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_07b"></a>REQ_WKUP_07b | <br>An enabled detector in mode 1 (Negedge) shall detect only at a fall of the forwarded value. <br><br>*Sources:* [`WKUP_DETECTOR.MODE`](../registers.md#wkup_detector)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup`: *no false cause*. In mode 1, the check allows a hardware write of 1 to a cause bit at 0 only when the write answers a fall of the forwarded value. A detector that detects on a rise therefore fails the check.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_08a"></a>REQ_WKUP_08a | <br>An enabled detector in mode 2 (Edge) shall detect every change of the forwarded value. <br><br>*Sources:* [`WKUP_DETECTOR.MODE`](../registers.md#wkup_detector)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup`: *edge sets cause*. The check requires a hardware write of 1 to bit `w` within 8 cycles of each rise and each fall of the forwarded value, if `EN` is 1 at the edge and in each of the 4 cycles after it.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_08b"></a>REQ_WKUP_08b | <br>An enabled detector in mode 2 (Edge) shall detect only at a change of the forwarded value. <br><br>*Sources:* [`WKUP_DETECTOR.MODE`](../registers.md#wkup_detector)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup`: *no false cause*. In mode 2, the check allows a hardware write of 1 to a cause bit at 0 only when the write answers a rise or a fall of the forwarded value.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_09a"></a>REQ_WKUP_09a | <br>An enabled detector in mode 3 (TimedHigh) shall detect when the forwarded value has been 1 for `CNT_TH` consecutive cycles. <br><br>*Sources:* [`WKUP_DETECTOR.MODE`](../registers.md#wkup_detector); [`WKUP_DETECTOR_CNT_TH.TH`](../registers.md#wkup_detector_cnt_th); [Wakeup Detectors](../theory_of_operation.md#wakeup-detectors); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `wkup_cnt_width`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup`: *timed high sets cause*. The check requires the cause bit to be set within 8 cycles of the cycle in which the forwarded value has been 1 for `CNT_TH` + 1 consecutive cycles with `EN` 1. The sources of this count differ by one cycle, so a detection after `CNT_TH` + 1 cycles also passes.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_09b"></a>REQ_WKUP_09b | <br>An enabled detector in mode 3 (TimedHigh) shall never detect while the forwarded value has been 1 for fewer than `CNT_TH` consecutive cycles. <br><br>*Sources:* [`WKUP_DETECTOR.MODE`](../registers.md#wkup_detector); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `wkup_cnt_width`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup`: *no false cause*. In mode 3, the check refuses a set of the cause bit while the forwarded value has been 1 for fewer than `CNT_TH` consecutive cycles with `EN` 1.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_10a"></a>REQ_WKUP_10a | <br>An enabled detector in mode 4 (TimedLow) shall detect when the forwarded value has been 0 for `CNT_TH` consecutive cycles. <br><br>*Sources:* [`WKUP_DETECTOR.MODE`](../registers.md#wkup_detector); [`WKUP_DETECTOR_CNT_TH.TH`](../registers.md#wkup_detector_cnt_th); [Wakeup Detectors](../theory_of_operation.md#wakeup-detectors); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `wkup_cnt_width`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup`: *timed low sets cause*. The check requires the cause bit to be set within 8 cycles of the cycle in which the forwarded value has been 0 for `CNT_TH` + 1 consecutive cycles with `EN` 1. The sources of this count differ by one cycle, so a detection after `CNT_TH` + 1 cycles also passes.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_10b"></a>REQ_WKUP_10b | <br>An enabled detector in mode 4 (TimedLow) shall never detect while the forwarded value has been 0 for fewer than `CNT_TH` consecutive cycles. <br><br>*Sources:* [`WKUP_DETECTOR.MODE`](../registers.md#wkup_detector); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `wkup_cnt_width`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup`: *no false cause*. In mode 4, the check refuses a set of the cause bit while the forwarded value has been 0 for fewer than `CNT_TH` consecutive cycles with `EN` 1.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_11"></a>REQ_WKUP_11 | <br>An enabled detector in mode 5, 6 or 7 shall detect exactly the rises of the forwarded value, as in mode 0 (Posedge). <br><br>*Sources:* [`WKUP_DETECTOR.MODE`](../registers.md#wkup_detector)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup`: *posedge sets cause*, *no false cause*. Both checks read `MODE` 5, 6 and 7 as mode 0.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_12"></a>REQ_WKUP_12 | <br>A detector whose enable is 0 shall detect nothing. <br><br>*Sources:* [`WKUP_DETECTOR_EN.EN`](../registers.md#wkup_detector_en)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup`: *no false cause*. The check allows a hardware write of 1 to a cause bit at 0 only when the write answers a pattern with `EN` 1. An edge within 4 cycles of a change of `EN` may set the cause bit or not.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_13a"></a>REQ_WKUP_13a | <br>The sampling of a detector's watched signal shall run while the detector is disabled, so that enabling the detector does not restart the sampling. (*RTL*) <br><br>*Sources:* No specification states this rule: the theory of operation and the register description are silent.<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup`: *no false cause*, *posedge sets cause*. The checks sample the pad at every value of `EN`. A pad that is already 1 before the enable therefore gives no rise, and a rise immediately after the enable counts as a rise.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_13b"></a>REQ_WKUP_13b | <br>A detector's filter shall run while the detector is disabled, so that enabling the detector does not restart the filter. (*RTL*) <br><br>*Sources:* No specification states this rule: the theory of operation and the register description are silent.<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup`: *no false cause*, *posedge sets cause*. The filter of the checks runs at every value of `EN`. A rise whose 4 samples span the enable counts as a rise, and a pad that is already 1 before the enable does not.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_14"></a>REQ_WKUP_14 | <br>When detector `w` is enabled and detects, bit `w` of `WKUP_CAUSE` shall become 1. <br><br>*Sources:* [Wakeup Detectors](../theory_of_operation.md#wakeup-detectors); [`WKUP_CAUSE.CAUSE`](../registers.md#wkup_cause); [Wakeup Features](../programmers_guide.md#wakeup-features); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `n_wkup_detect`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup`: *posedge sets cause*, *negedge sets cause*, *edge sets cause*, *timed high sets cause*, *timed low sets cause*, *set lands*. The checks require a cause set within 8 cycles of each detection, and the cause bit to be 1 in the cycle after the set.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_15"></a>REQ_WKUP_15 | <br>Bit `w` of `WKUP_CAUSE` shall become 1 only when detector `w` detects. <br><br>*Sources:* [`WKUP_CAUSE.CAUSE`](../registers.md#wkup_cause); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `n_wkup_detect`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup`: *no false cause*, *cause rises only on a set*. The checks allow a cause set of bit `w` only on a pattern of detector `w`, and allow bit `w` to rise only in the cycle after a cause set of that bit.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_16"></a>REQ_WKUP_16 | <br>Once a cause bit (a bit of `WKUP_CAUSE`) is 1, it shall stay 1 until software's clear of that bit lands. This holds for any behaviour of the bit's detector, of the detector's enable and of the pad that the detector watches. <br><br>*Sources:* [`WKUP_CAUSE`](../registers.md#wkup_cause); [`WKUP_CAUSE.CAUSE`](../registers.md#wkup_cause); [Wakeup Features](../programmers_guide.md#wakeup-features); [Wakeup Recording](../../../pwrmgr/doc/theory_of_operation.md#wakeup-recording)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup_cause`: *cause holds until cleared*, *hardware only sets cause*. The checks allow the cause bit to fall only in the cycle after a landed clear, or at `rst_aon_ni`. The checks allow a hardware write of 0 to the bit only in a cycle in which a clear lands.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_17"></a>REQ_WKUP_17 | <br>When a detection sets a cause bit in the cycle in which software's clear of that bit lands, the bit shall end at 0. Software's clear has precedence over the detection. <br><br>*Sources:* [`WKUP_CAUSE`](../registers.md#wkup_cause); [Type RW](../../../../../../util/reggen/README.md#type-rw); [Simultaneous SW and HW access](../../../../../../util/reggen/README.md#simultaneous-sw-and-hw-access)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup_cause`: *set and clear together*. When a hardware write of 1 to a cause bit comes in the cycle in which a clear of that bit lands, the check requires the bit to be 0 in the next cycle.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_18a"></a>REQ_WKUP_18a | <br>`pin_wkup_req_o` shall be 1 while at least one cause bit is 1. <br><br>*Sources:* [Inter-Module Signals](../interfaces.md#inter-module-signals); [Wakeup Detectors](../theory_of_operation.md#wakeup-detectors); [Wakeup Recording](../../../pwrmgr/doc/theory_of_operation.md#wakeup-recording); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `n_wkup_detect`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup_cause`: *request follows cause*. The check requires `pin_wkup_req_o` to be 1 within 2 cycles of any cause bit rising. The check also requires the request to be 1 while any cause bit has been 1 for 3 consecutive cycles.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_18b"></a>REQ_WKUP_18b | <br>`pin_wkup_req_o` shall be 0 while every cause bit is 0. <br><br>*Sources:* [Inter-Module Signals](../interfaces.md#inter-module-signals); [Wakeup Detectors](../theory_of_operation.md#wakeup-detectors); [Wakeup Recording](../../../pwrmgr/doc/theory_of_operation.md#wakeup-recording); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `n_wkup_detect`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup_cause`: *no request without cause*. The check requires `pin_wkup_req_o` to be 0 once every cause bit has been 0 for 3 consecutive cycles.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_19a"></a>REQ_WKUP_19a | <br>A write to a detector's enable shall reach the AON logic within a bounded time. <br><br>*Sources:* [`WKUP_DETECTOR_EN`](../registers.md#wkup_detector_en); [Wakeup Features](../programmers_guide.md#wakeup-features)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup_sync`: *write lands*, *no change without a write*. For `EN`, the checks require each accepted write to land within 4 cycles, and allow only a write that has not yet landed to change the field.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_19b"></a>REQ_WKUP_19b | <br>A write to a detector's configuration shall reach the AON logic within a bounded time. <br><br>*Sources:* [`WKUP_DETECTOR`](../registers.md#wkup_detector); [Wakeup Features](../programmers_guide.md#wakeup-features)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup_sync`: *write lands*, *no change without a write*. For `MODE`, `FILTER` and `MIODIO`, the checks require each accepted write to land within 4 cycles, and allow only a write that has not yet landed to change one of these fields.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_19c"></a>REQ_WKUP_19c | <br>A write to a detector's threshold shall reach the AON logic within a bounded time. <br><br>*Sources:* [`WKUP_DETECTOR_CNT_TH`](../registers.md#wkup_detector_cnt_th); [Wakeup Features](../programmers_guide.md#wakeup-features)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup_sync`: *write lands*, *no change without a write*. For `CNT_TH`, the checks require each accepted write to land within 4 cycles, and allow only a write that has not yet landed to change the field.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_19d"></a>REQ_WKUP_19d | <br>A clear of `WKUP_CAUSE` shall reach the AON logic within a bounded time. <br><br>*Sources:* [`WKUP_CAUSE`](../registers.md#wkup_cause); [Wakeup Features](../programmers_guide.md#wakeup-features)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup_sync`: *clear lands*, *landed clear clears cause*, *no clear without a write*. The checks require each software clear of the cause bit to land within 10 cycles, and the bit to be 0 one cycle later. The checks allow no clear to land without a software clear.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_20a"></a>REQ_WKUP_20a | <br>A write to a detector's configuration that is accepted before a write to its enable shall hold in the AON logic no later than that enable write does. <br><br>*Sources:* [`WKUP_DETECTOR_EN`](../registers.md#wkup_detector_en)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup_sync`: *writes land in order*. The check compares the AON cycles in which a configuration write and a later enable write land. The check fails when the enable write lands first.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_wkup_20b"></a>REQ_WKUP_20b | <br>A write to a detector's threshold that is accepted before a write to its enable shall hold in the AON logic no later than that enable write does. <br><br>*Sources:* [`WKUP_DETECTOR_EN`](../registers.md#wkup_detector_en)<br><br>*Checked by:* `wakeup_sync`: *writes land in order*. The check compares the AON cycles in which a threshold write and a later enable write land. An enable that holds before the threshold fails the check.<br><br> |
% if usb:

${"##"} USB wakeup

Conditions:

* The module is usbdev's AON/Wake module (${'' if topname in 'earlgrey englishbreakfast'.split() else "Earl Grey's "}[USB Wakeup Detection Module](../../../../../top_${topname if topname in 'earlgrey englishbreakfast'.split() else 'earlgrey'}/ip_autogen/pinmux/doc/theory_of_operation.md#usb-wakeup-detection-module)), and each pinmux port in the table below has the role that usbdev's documents give to the port it connects to:

| Port | Role |
|---|---|
| `usbdev_dppullup_en` | D+ pull-up enable, routed to the module's input `usbdev_dppullup_en_i` ([AON/Wake Interface Pins](../../../../../ip/usbdev/doc/theory_of_operation.md#aonwake-interface-pins)) |
| `usbdev_dnpullup_en` | D- pull-up enable, routed to the module's input `usbdev_dnpullup_en_i` ([AON/Wake Interface Pins](../../../../../ip/usbdev/doc/theory_of_operation.md#aonwake-interface-pins)) |
| `usbdev_suspend_req` | "Request to activate the AON/Wake module" ([Inter-Module Signals](../../../../../ip/usbdev/doc/interfaces.md#inter-module-signals)) |
| `usbdev_wake_ack` | "Acknowledge a wake signal from the AON/Wake" ([Inter-Module Signals](../../../../../ip/usbdev/doc/interfaces.md#inter-module-signals)) |
| `usb_dppullup_en` | D+ pull-up enable from the module's output `usb_dppullup_en_o` ([AON/Wake Interface Pins](../../../../../ip/usbdev/doc/theory_of_operation.md#aonwake-interface-pins)) |
| `usb_dnpullup_en` | D- pull-up enable from the module's output `usb_dnpullup_en_o` ([AON/Wake Interface Pins](../../../../../ip/usbdev/doc/theory_of_operation.md#aonwake-interface-pins)) |
| `usbdev_bus_not_idle` | The event "the USB is in a non-idle state" ([Inter-Module Signals](../../../../../ip/usbdev/doc/interfaces.md#inter-module-signals)) |
| `usbdev_bus_reset` | The event "a USB Bus Reset occurred" ([Inter-Module Signals](../../../../../ip/usbdev/doc/interfaces.md#inter-module-signals)) |
| `usbdev_sense_lost` | The event "the VBUS/SENSE signal became deasserted" ([Inter-Module Signals](../../../../../ip/usbdev/doc/interfaces.md#inter-module-signals)) |
| `usbdev_wake_detect_active` | The active state, "the external AON/Wake module is active" ([Inter-Module Signals](../../../../../ip/usbdev/doc/interfaces.md#inter-module-signals)), from the module's output `wake_detect_active_aon_o` ([AON/Wake Interface Pins](../../../../../ip/usbdev/doc/theory_of_operation.md#aonwake-interface-pins)) |

* A suspend request is a `clk_aon_i` cycle in which `usbdev_suspend_req` is 1, and a wake acknowledge is a `clk_aon_i` cycle in which `usbdev_wake_ack` is 1.
* The USB lines are the module's D+, D- and VBUS inputs, and they may take any value at any time.
* The module is active while `usbdev_wake_detect_active` is 1.
* The bus is idle while each line equals its own pad pull-up enable: D+ equals `usb_dppullup_en`, and D- equals `usb_dnpullup_en`.
* With no enable at 1, both lines low is idle, so a bus reset then sets the bus reset event but not the bus-not-idle event.
* The two pull-up rows [`REQ_USB_06d`](#req_usb_06d) and [`REQ_USB_06e`](#req_usb_06e) hold when usbdev keeps its pull-up enables unchanged in the 4 always-on clock cycles before a suspend request.
* The wiring rows follow usbdev's documents; the pinmux theory of operation names only `usb_wkup_req` (${'' if topname in 'earlgrey englishbreakfast'.split() else "Earl Grey's "}[USB Wakeup Detection Module](../../../../../top_${topname if topname in 'earlgrey englishbreakfast'.split() else 'earlgrey'}/ip_autogen/pinmux/doc/theory_of_operation.md#usb-wakeup-detection-module)).
* The rows name each signal as these documents do, and the port of the pinmux has the same name with the suffix `_i` or `_o`.

Testpoints: [`usb_conn`](../../data/pinmux_env_fpv_testplan.hjson#usb_conn) (V2, priority 2), [`usb_module`](../../data/pinmux_env_fpv_testplan.hjson#usb_module) (V2, priority 2).

| ID | Requirement |
|---|---|
| <a id="req_usb_01a"></a>REQ_USB_01a | <br>`usbdev_dppullup_en` shall reach the module's input that has the same role, unchanged and in the same cycle. <br><br>*Sources:* [Inter-Module Signals](../interfaces.md#inter-module-signals); [top_${topname}.hjson${eg(' `inter_module.connect usbdev.usb_dp_pullup`')}](../../../../data/top_${topname}.hjson); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `enable_usb_wakeup`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson)<br><br>*Checked by:* `usb_conn`: *D+ pull-up enable in*. The check compares the module's `usbdev_dppullup_en_i` port with `usbdev_dppullup_en` in every cycle.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_usb_01b"></a>REQ_USB_01b | <br>`usbdev_dnpullup_en` shall reach the module's input that has the same role, unchanged and in the same cycle. <br><br>*Sources:* [Inter-Module Signals](../interfaces.md#inter-module-signals); [top_${topname}.hjson${eg(' `inter_module.connect usbdev.usb_dn_pullup`')}](../../../../data/top_${topname}.hjson); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `enable_usb_wakeup`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson)<br><br>*Checked by:* `usb_conn`: *D- pull-up enable in*. The check compares the module's `usbdev_dnpullup_en_i` port with `usbdev_dnpullup_en` in every cycle.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_usb_01c"></a>REQ_USB_01c | <br>`usbdev_suspend_req` shall reach the module's input that has the same role, unchanged and in the same cycle. <br><br>*Sources:* [Inter-Module Signals](../interfaces.md#inter-module-signals); [top_${topname}.hjson${eg(' `inter_module.connect usbdev.usb_aon_suspend_req`')}](../../../../data/top_${topname}.hjson); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `enable_usb_wakeup`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson)<br><br>*Checked by:* `usb_conn`: *suspend request in*. The check compares the module's suspend request port with `usbdev_suspend_req` in every cycle. The comparison also covers a one-cycle pulse.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_usb_01d"></a>REQ_USB_01d | <br>`usbdev_wake_ack` shall reach the module's input that has the same role, unchanged and in the same cycle. <br><br>*Sources:* [Inter-Module Signals](../interfaces.md#inter-module-signals); [top_${topname}.hjson${eg(' `inter_module.connect usbdev.usb_aon_wake_ack`')}](../../../../data/top_${topname}.hjson); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `enable_usb_wakeup`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson)<br><br>*Checked by:* `usb_conn`: *wake acknowledge in*. The check compares the module's wake acknowledge port with `usbdev_wake_ack` in every cycle.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_usb_02a"></a>REQ_USB_02a | <br>`usb_dppullup_en` shall carry the module's pull-up enable that has the same role, unchanged and in the same cycle. <br><br>*Sources:* [Inter-Module Signals](../interfaces.md#inter-module-signals); [top_${topname}.hjson${eg(' `inter_module.external pinmux.usb_dppullup_en`')}](../../../../data/top_${topname}.hjson); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `enable_usb_wakeup`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson)<br><br>*Checked by:* `usb_conn`: *D+ pull-up enable out*. The check compares `usb_dppullup_en` with the module's `usb_dppullup_en_o` port in every cycle.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_usb_02b"></a>REQ_USB_02b | <br>`usb_dnpullup_en` shall carry the module's pull-up enable that has the same role, unchanged and in the same cycle. <br><br>*Sources:* [Inter-Module Signals](../interfaces.md#inter-module-signals); [top_${topname}.hjson${eg(' `inter_module.external pinmux.usb_dnpullup_en`')}](../../../../data/top_${topname}.hjson); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `enable_usb_wakeup`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson)<br><br>*Checked by:* `usb_conn`: *D- pull-up enable out*. The check compares `usb_dnpullup_en` with the module's `usb_dnpullup_en_o` port in every cycle.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_usb_02c"></a>REQ_USB_02c | <br>`usbdev_bus_not_idle` shall carry the one of the module's three events that has the same role, unchanged and in the same cycle. <br><br>*Sources:* [Inter-Module Signals](../interfaces.md#inter-module-signals); [top_${topname}.hjson${eg(' `inter_module.connect usbdev.usb_aon_bus_not_idle`')}](../../../../data/top_${topname}.hjson); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `enable_usb_wakeup`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson)<br><br>*Checked by:* `usb_conn`: *bus not idle out*. The check compares `usbdev_bus_not_idle` with the module's resume event port in every cycle.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_usb_02d"></a>REQ_USB_02d | <br>`usbdev_bus_reset` shall carry the one of the module's three events that has the same role, unchanged and in the same cycle. <br><br>*Sources:* [Inter-Module Signals](../interfaces.md#inter-module-signals); [top_${topname}.hjson${eg(' `inter_module.connect usbdev.usb_aon_bus_reset`')}](../../../../data/top_${topname}.hjson); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `enable_usb_wakeup`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson)<br><br>*Checked by:* `usb_conn`: *bus reset out*. The check compares `usbdev_bus_reset` with the module's bus reset event port in every cycle.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_usb_02e"></a>REQ_USB_02e | <br>`usbdev_sense_lost` shall carry the one of the module's three events that has the same role, unchanged and in the same cycle. <br><br>*Sources:* [Inter-Module Signals](../interfaces.md#inter-module-signals); [top_${topname}.hjson${eg(' `inter_module.connect usbdev.usb_aon_sense_lost`')}](../../../../data/top_${topname}.hjson); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `enable_usb_wakeup`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson)<br><br>*Checked by:* `usb_conn`: *sense lost out*. The check compares `usbdev_sense_lost` with the module's VBUS lost event port in every cycle.<br><br> |
| <a id="req_usb_02f"></a>REQ_USB_02f | <br>`usbdev_wake_detect_active` shall carry the module's active state, unchanged and in the same cycle. <br><br>*Sources:* [Inter-Module Signals](../interfaces.md#inter-module-signals); [top_${topname}.hjson${eg(' `inter_module.connect pinmux.usbdev_wake_detect_active`')}](../../../../data/top_${topname}.hjson); [top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson `enable_usb_wakeup`](../../data/top_${topname}_pinmux.ipconfig.hjson)<br><br>*Checked by:* `usb_conn`: *active out*. The check compares `usbdev_wake_detect_active` with the module's `wake_detect_active_aon_o` port in every cycle. A value taken from another port, or a delayed value, fails the check.<br><br> |
